** TODO A ThreadSanitizer spot-check note

How to run the suite under the sanitizer and read the table. The
build is a complete 15D with =CXXOPTS=-fsanitize=thread= and
=LDOPTS=-fsanitize=thread= on every stage after =config=, utilities
included, and =make runall= in its own =tests=. The harness's
address-space limit does not stop the sanitizer: it re-executes the
program without the limit and prints two warning lines, so every
=NOUT= row reads =FORMAT=. Six programs cannot run: =0.alloc=,
=0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=, =18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp=
jump out of signal handlers or test =setjmp=; the sanitizer's
=longjmp= interceptor finds no buffer for the jump, its check fails,
and the program exits 66, the sanitizer's own =exitcode= default.
The defect is the sanitizer runtime's against glibc 2.44, not the
tests' (the =WAITING= entry on =longjmp= under the sanitizer).
The address-space limit plays no part: the exit is the same without
it. =0.inputiter= and =0.outputiter= die with an empty tail because
they close =stderr= first, to silence the assertions they provoke,
and every write of the report returns =EBADF=; the other four print
the check. Measured with =strace= on aarch64; x86-64 exits 66 too.
Two cannot pass:
=18.exception= and =20.temp.buffer= expect =bad_alloc= from a huge
request, and the sanitizer's =operator new= reports out of memory
instead, =allocator_may_return_null= or not. =22.locale.codecvt=
times out at 60 seconds behind the instrumented =localedef=. One
report stays: =27.objects= shows a lock-order inversion, =cout='s
stream buffer mutex against its =ios_base= mutex. An insertion takes
the buffer's and then, on the first use of =fill=, the stream's;
=tie= takes the stream's (=src/ios.cpp:271=) and allocates the
user-data block under it, and the test's replacement =operator new=
inserts into =cout=, taking the buffer's. It deadlocks only with a
replacement =operator new= that writes to the stream while another
thread ties it. Won't fix (2026-10-13): the report is expected. The
rest of the table matches the plain one but for the reports. First run,
2026-10-13 on aarch64: 419 seconds, 274 programs, 12 non-zero exits.

